{
  "abstract": "We obtain an explicit discriminant bound for imaginary quadratic fields whose full ideal class-group exponent divides $16$ by converting local rank restrictions into minimum prime products. For a small block of positive prime discriminants, a hypothetical upper bound on the class-number saving limits which subsets and diagonal completions can occur. Exact minimum products for each admissible cardinality combine across disjoint blocks, forcing larger saving when they exceed the current discriminant cap. A joint radical inequality retains the ordinary sign contribution while paying for directions lost from a higher-pairing kernel. We express the resulting positive score as the sum of two principal matrix ranks, using a fixed Gaussian matrix for the Rédei symmetrization, including overlapping labels and the positive dyadic coordinate. At the exceptional dyadic type $-4$, we retain its radical hyperplane and an admissible kernel. Under the imported inequality $L(1,\\chi_{-D})>1/(u+4\\log u+83)$ for $u=\\log D\\ge10$, the complete certificate proves $\\omega(D)\\le66$ and $\\log D<336.095$. The exact ordinary comparison on the same prime pools gives $\\omega(D)\\le68$ and $\\log D<348.628$. Separate author implementations exhaust the block diagonals, verify integer product obstructions and certify all numerical caps with directed interval arithmetic; the replay includes complete initial population coverage. The analytic inequality and classical class-group theory remain imported. The Gaussian matrix computes only the higher symmetrization. No new reciprocity law, census, classification or feasibility of enumerating the remaining range is claimed.",
